Relevant columns and their possible usage for the requested player‑level statistics  

player  
• player_id ­– unique identifier to join with every other table.  
• player_name ­– display name.  
• batting_hand ­– show batting style.  
• bowling_skill ­– show bowling style.

player_match  
• player_id ­– links a player to a match.  
• match_id  – lets us count matches played.  
• role – figure out the player’s most‑frequent role (Captain, Keeper, …).  
• team_id – can be handy to know which side the player represented in a match.

ball_by_ball  
• match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no – composite key to join with batsman_scored / wicket_taken.  
• striker – the batter on strike for the ball (used to total runs, balls faced, highest score, 30/50/100 counts).  
• bowler – bowler who delivered the ball (used for wickets taken and runs conceded).  

batsman_scored  
• match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no – join key to ball_by_ball.  
• runs_scored – runs credited to the striker (sum for total runs, highest score, 30+/50+/100+ checks).

wicket_taken  
• match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no – join key to ball_by_ball.  
• player_out – player dismissed (count to get dismissals).  
• kind_out – can filter if you want to exclude ‘run out’ from bowler wickets.  

Conditions often required  
• JOIN ball_by_ball USING (match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no) to pull batter / bowler.  
• WHERE striker = player_id  (for batting stats).  
• WHERE bowler = player_id  (for bowling stats).  
• WHERE player_out = player_id (for dismissals).  
• GROUP BY player_id (for per‑player aggregates).  
• COUNT(DISTINCT match_id) (for matches played).  
• SUM(runs_scored) (for total runs) etc.  
• HAVING … (to pick “best performance” per player).

Below are ten exploratory SQLite queries (each limited to 20 rows) that gradually surface the data you would need.  
Every query is different and annotated.

```sql
-- 1. Description: Find all distinct player roles that appear in the player_match table
SELECT DISTINCT role
FROM player_match
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Sample of players with how many matches they appear in player_match
SELECT player_id,
       COUNT(DISTINCT match_id) AS matches_played
FROM player_match
GROUP BY player_id
ORDER BY matches_played DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Show different batting‑hand strings that exist
SELECT DISTINCT batting_hand
FROM player
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Show distribution of single‑ball run values recorded in batsman_scored
SELECT DISTINCT runs_scored
FROM batsman_scored
ORDER BY runs_scored
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: First 20 rows linking striker with the runs he scored on that ball
SELECT b.match_id,
       b.over_id,
       b.ball_id,
       b.striker,
       s.runs_scored
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
JOIN batsman_scored AS s
  ON b.match_id = s.match_id
 AND b.over_id  = s.over_id
 AND b.ball_id  = s.ball_id
 AND b.innings_no = s.innings_no
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Distinct kinds of dismissal captured in wicket_taken
SELECT DISTINCT kind_out
FROM wicket_taken
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Sample of wickets credited to each bowler (joining wicket_taken to know which bowler got the wicket)
SELECT b.bowler,
       COUNT(*) AS wickets
FROM wicket_taken AS w
JOIN ball_by_ball AS b
  ON b.match_id = w.match_id
 AND b.over_id  = w.over_id
 AND b.ball_id  = w.ball_id
 AND b.innings_no = w.innings_no
GROUP BY b.bowler
ORDER BY wickets DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Total balls faced per player (every row where the player is striker counts as one ball faced)
SELECT striker AS player_id,
       COUNT(*) AS balls_faced
FROM ball_by_ball
GROUP BY striker
ORDER BY balls_faced DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Total runs per player in a single match (helps later to get highest‑score, 30/50/100 counts)
SELECT b.striker  AS player_id,
       b.match_id,
       SUM(s.runs_scored) AS runs_in_match
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
JOIN batsman_scored AS s
  ON b.match_id = s.match_id
 AND b.over_id  = s.over_id
 AND b.ball_id  = s.ball_id
 AND b.innings_no = s.innings_no
GROUP BY b.striker, b.match_id
ORDER BY runs_in_match DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Runs conceded and wickets taken by a bowler in each match (basis for economy rate and best bowling)
SELECT b.bowler          AS player_id,
       b.match_id,
       SUM(s.runs_scored)                         AS runs_conceded_off_bat,
       COUNT(w.player_out)                        AS wickets_taken
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
JOIN batsman_scored AS s
  ON b.match_id   = s.match_id
 AND b.over_id    = s.over_id
 AND b.ball_id    = s.ball_id
 AND b.innings_no = s.innings_no
LEFT JOIN wicket_taken AS w
  ON w.match_id   = b.match_id
 AND w.over_id    = b.over_id
 AND w.ball_id    = b.ball_id
 AND w.innings_no = b.innings_no
GROUP BY b.bowler, b.match_id
ORDER BY wickets_taken DESC, runs_conceded_off_bat
LIMIT 20;
```

These exploratory queries expose every piece of information needed to build the comprehensive per‑player summary (most‑frequent role, runs, dismissals, averages, strike rate, wickets, economy, best bowling, etc.).
